    <h1>Long Bien Bridge Motorbike Riding Guide</h1>
    <p>Of all the structures a visitor on a rented motorbike can ride across in Hanoi, none carries the weight of Long Bien Bridge. The old cantilever spans over the Red River have carried trains, carts, bicycles and motorbikes for more than a century, and riding its deck remains one of the city's signature two-wheeled experiences. This guide covers what the bridge means to a rider: the approaches from both sides, the surface and traffic character, and how the crossing stitches the Long Bien district to the historic center.</p>
    <p>The bridge is close to home ground for us: our shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu sits in Long Bien, on the river's east bank, and the bridge is a familiar part of our daily riding territory. It pairs naturally with the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/">Old Quarter motorbike rental</a> hub, since the southern approach begins within reach of the Old Quarter streets, and with the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/west-lake-motorbike-ride-hanoi/">West Lake ride</a> for riders building a loop of the city's water landmarks.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
